#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:1
msgid "Source Installation on Windows 10 or 11"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:4
msgid "Windows support on Rotary is highly experimental"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:7
msgid ""
"OGRE2 rendering capabilities are supported in Windows, and Gazebo GUI works "
"in a limited fashion. These functionalities correspond to the currently "
"building packages `gz-rendering` and `gz-sim`, respectively. The packages "
"will build, without any failures when using their functionalities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:12
msgid ""
"**NOTE** You should be able to use `ogre` as a rendering engine instead of "
"the default `ogre2`. Just append `--render-engine ogre` to the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:16
msgid "Dependency distribution (conda-forge) and the package manager (Pixi)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:18
msgid ""
"Binaries for all the dependencies used by Gazebo can be found in the [conda-"
"forge](https://conda-forge.org/) package repository. The Gazebo buildfarm "
"and these instructions uses the [Pixi](https://pixi.sh/) package manager but "
"other package managers for Conda should work like miniforge, mamba, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:22
msgid "Install dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:24
msgid ""
"If no package manager is installed in the system to manage conda-forge "
"dependencies, the recommended option is to follow the Pixi installer "
"instructions from https://pixi.sh/latest/ to install pixi. Once pixi has "
"been installed, close the terminal session and start it again, which will "
"ensure pixi is on the PATH."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:29
msgid ""
"Install [Visual Studio 2019 or 2022](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/"
"downloads/). The Community version is free for students, open-source "
"developers, and personal development. Check \"Desktop development with C++\" "
"in the Workloads tab, check \"MFC and ATL support\", and uncheck \"C++ Cmake "
"Tools.\" We will install cmake via Conda. All other checkboxes can be left "
"unchecked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:35
msgid ""
"Open a Visual Studio Command Prompt (search for \"x64 Native Tools Command "
"Prompt for VS\" in the Windows search field near the Windows button) or "
"Developer PowerShell for VS (search for \"developer powershell\"). "
"Optionally, right-click and pin to the task bar for quick access in the "
"future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:40
msgid ""
"Create the Pixi project directory and download the configuration files Pixi "
"projects operates inside a given directory, creating one in any user system "
"location should be enough. There are two configuration files to download:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:43
msgid ""
"mkdir gazebo\n"
"cd gazebo\n"
"curl.exe -L -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gazebo-tooling/release-"
"tools/refs/heads/master/conda/envs/noble_like/pixi.toml\n"
"curl.exe -L -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gazebo-tooling/release-"
"tools/refs/heads/master/conda/envs/noble_like/pixi.lock\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:49
msgid ""
"Install dependencies using Pixi and enable the Pixi environment: Once inside "
"the Pixi project directory (i.e: `gazebo`), Pixi can install the "
"dependencies that are part of the configuration file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:52
msgid ""
":: inside the gazebo directory\n"
"pixi install\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:56
msgid ""
"This can take some minutes. After this Pixi can make accessible all the "
"libraries and dependencies installed by providing a shell environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:58
msgid ""
":: inside the gazebo directory\n"
"pixi shell\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:62
msgid ""
"With the Pixi shell environment active (there will be a \"(gazebo)\" label "
"before the terminal prompt) the Pixi environment is accessible from anywhere "
"in the file system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:65
msgid ""
"Navigate to where you would like to build the library, create and enter your "
"workspace directory, create the `src` directory which will contain the "
"Gazebo source code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:67
msgid ""
"mkdir gz-ws\n"
"cd gz-ws\n"
"mkdir src\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:73
msgid "Then clone the repositories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:74
msgid ""
"vcs import --input https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gazebo-tooling/"
"gazebodistro/master/collection-rotary.yaml src/\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:78
msgid "Building the Gazebo Libraries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:81
msgid ""
"Be sure of being under a Visual Studio Developer shell (step 3 above) and "
"after executing the pixi shell (step 5 above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:85
msgid ""
"Once the compiler and all the sources are in place it is time to compile "
"them. Start the procedure by navigating to your workspace and listing the "
"packages recognized by `colcon`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:89
msgid "colcon graph\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:93
msgid ""
"`colcon graph` should list the Gazebo libraries with an [interdependency "
"diagram](https://colcon.readthedocs.io/en/released/reference/verb/graph."
"html#example-output). If that is the case, then you are ready to build the "
"whole set of libraries:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:97
msgid ""
"colcon build --cmake-args -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF -DSKIP_SWIG=ON --merge-install "
"--packages-up-to gz-sim gz-tools\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:100
msgid "Tests are turned off as they are not currently supported on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:102
msgid "To build a specific package with all its dependent packages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:104
msgid "colcon build --merge-install --packages-up-to PACKAGE_NAME\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:108
msgid "To build a single package:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:110
msgid "colcon build --packages-select PACKAGE_NAME\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:114
msgid ""
"Visit [colcon documentation](https://colcon.readthedocs.io/en/released/#) to "
"view more `colcon` build and test options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:116
msgid "If there are no errors, all the binaries should be ready to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:118
msgid "Using the workspace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:120
msgid ""
"The workspace needs to be sourced every time a new terminal is used ( and "
"the Pixi environment activated before that)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:123
msgid ""
"The overall instructions for setting up a new terminal to use the built "
"workspace are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:126
msgid ""
"# CMD\n"
"cd gazebo\n"
"pixi shell\n"
"call gz-ws\\install\\setup.bat\n"
"\n"
"# PowerShell\n"
"cd gazebo\n"
"pixi shell\n"
".\\gz-ws\\install\\setup.ps1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:137
msgid "You should now be able to launch gazebo normally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:139
msgid "gz sim --verbose\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:143
msgid ""
"Alternativally launching the server and the client in two different "
"terminales (after sourcing in both the install scripts, the pixi shell and "
"the QT env variables):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:146
msgid ""
"# Launch server in one terminal\n"
"gz sim -s\n"
"\n"
"# In separate terminal, launch gui\n"
"gz sim -g\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:154
msgid ""
"This is the end of the source install instructions; head back to the "
"[Getting started](getstarted) page to start using Gazebo!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:157
msgid ""
"**NOTE** If your username contains spaces (which is quite common on "
"Windows), you will probably get errors  saying `Invalid partition name "
"[Computer:My User With Spaces]`. Fix this by changing `GZ_PARTITION`  to "
"something else:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:161
msgid "set GZ_PARTITION=test\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:164
msgid "Remember to set the same partition in all other consoles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:166
msgid "Uninstalling source-based install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:168
msgid ""
"A source-based install can be \"uninstalled\" using several methods, "
"depending on the results you want:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:171
msgid ""
"If you installed your workspace with `colcon` as instructed above, "
"\"uninstalling\" could be just a matter of opening a new terminal and not "
"sourcing the workspace's `setup.bat`. This way, your environment will behave "
"as though Gazebo is not installed on your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:176
msgid ""
"If, in addition to not wanting to use the libraries, you're also trying to "
"free up space, you can delete the entire workspace directory from within "
"your `conda` environment with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:180
msgid ""
"rmdir /s /q <workspace_name>  # gz-ws was used as workspace_name in this "
"document\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:184
msgid ""
"If you want to keep the source code, you can remove the `install` / "
"`build` / `log` directories as desired, leaving the `src` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:187
msgid ""
"Last, if you do not need the Pixi environment anymore, you can remove it with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:189
msgid ""
"rmdir /s /q <pixi_env_path>  # \"gazebo\" was used as pixi_env_path in this "
"document\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:193
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:195
msgid "See [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#windows)"
msgstr ""
